\title[Berggren moves on $(\ZZ/m)^3$]{Berggren Moves on $(\ZZ/m)^3$:\\
Exact Classification, Seed Recovery,\\ and an Information-Theoretic Modulus Separation}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
The three Berggren (Barning--Hall) moves generate a free ternary tree on the primitive Pythagorean triples, rooted at $(3,4,5)$. We exhibit an exact linear classifier
\[
\which(a,b,c) = \begin{cases} B_1 & 5a < 3c,\\ B_2 & 3c \le 5a < 4c,\\ B_3 & 4c \le 5a,\end{cases}
\]
and prove it simultaneously sound and complete: $\which(B_i v) = i$ for every positive Pythagorean triple $v$ and every $i$. Combined with the explicit integer inverses $B_i^{-1} = QB_i^{\mathsf T}Q$, $Q = \diag(1,1,-1)$, this yields an $O(k)$ algorithm recovering an entire control word from a single observed state, and shows the Berggren monoid acts freely on the positive cone, so the length-$k$ state space has exactly $3^k$ points.

Pushing the system through the reduction $\ZZ^3 \to (\Zm)^3$, reduction is equivariant, every modular move is a bijection, and the Lorentz form $a^2+b^2-c^2$ stays invariant; the classifier remains \emph{sound} on every state whose hypotenuse is below the modulus, and this hypothesis is sharp (explicit failure at $m=7$). Nevertheless the seed-recovery problem undergoes a sharp phase change. We prove: (i) if $5\cdot7^k < m$ then a modular observer recovers every word of length $\le k$; (ii) if $m^3 < 3^k$ then \emph{no function} of the observation recovers the word; (iii) for every $n$ with $m^3 n < 3^k$ some observation is consistent with more than $n$ words, so the ambiguity is $\Omega(3^k/m^3)$; (iv) every reachable state is a primitive null vector, so modulo a prime $p$ the observation lies on a cone of at most $2p^2$ points, improving the threshold to $2p^2 < 3^k$ and the ambiguity to $\Omega(3^k/2p^2)$; (v) recovery for all words of length $\le k$ solves the discrete logarithm for $B_2$ in $\GL_3(\Zm)$, which via the spectral identity $(B_2+I)(B_2^2-6B_2+I)=0$, with silver-ratio roots $3\pm2\sqrt2 = (1\pm\sqrt2)^2$, is identified with index-finding for the negative Pell equation $x^2-2y^2=-1$.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Berggren moves, Pythagorean tree, Lorentz form, seed recovery, discrete logarithm, Pell equation, silver ratio, information-theoretic hardness}

